WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.821
System scope is the foundation for evidence.

2
00:00:02.821 --> 00:00:17.743
The cloud service offering must be described clearly enough that reviewers understand what is included, what is excluded, which environments matter, which dependencies support the service, and where customer responsibilities begin.

3
00:00:17.743 --> 00:00:21.381
Boundary documentation is not just a compliance diagram.

4
00:00:21.381 --> 00:00:27.395
It determines which controls need evidence and which systems are relevant to the risk decision.

5
00:00:27.395 --> 00:00:44.395
A practical boundary description includes application components, infrastructure, data stores, management interfaces, identity systems, network paths, external services, support tooling, build and deployment processes, and monitoring systems as appropriate.

6
00:00:44.395 --> 00:00:57.239
It should also describe data flows, administrative access paths, customer-managed settings, inherited platform controls, and dependencies that affect confidentiality, integrity, or availability.

7
00:00:57.239 --> 00:01:00.134
Scope should be visible in words and diagrams.

8
00:01:00.134 --> 00:01:03.846
Included and excluded components need careful treatment.

9
00:01:03.846 --> 00:01:13.201
If a component is excluded, the documentation should make clear why it is outside the cloud service offering and whether it still affects the service.

10
00:01:13.201 --> 00:01:21.293
If an external service is relied upon, the team should explain the relationship and any inherited or shared control assumptions.

11
00:01:21.293 --> 00:01:27.158
Ambiguity creates gaps, especially when multiple teams assume someone else is responsible.

12
00:01:27.158 --> 00:01:30.721
Unclear scope creates assessment and operational risk.

13
00:01:30.721 --> 00:01:33.765
Evidence may be collected for the wrong systems.

14
00:01:33.765 --> 00:01:36.586
Control narratives may overstate coverage.

15
00:01:36.586 --> 00:01:39.778
Customer responsibilities may be misunderstood.

16
00:01:39.778 --> 00:01:43.713
Vulnerabilities or incidents may be routed to the wrong owner.

17
00:01:43.713 --> 00:01:55.146
A clear authorization boundary helps assessors, reviewers, engineers, and operations teams evaluate the service consistently and act on the same understanding of what is in scope.
